WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.940
You roll up your sleeve
for a vaccine shot.

2
00:00:02.940 --> 00:00:05.280
Your arm is sore for a day.

3
00:00:05.280 --> 00:00:10.390
But each vaccinated person also lowers
the chance that others catch the disease.

4
00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:15.820
For measles, health agencies aim for
95 percent coverage with 2 doses.

5
00:00:15.820 --> 00:00:21.070
Worldwide in 2023, second-dose
coverage was 74 percent.

6
00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:24.760
So if a vaccine also protects
the people around you,

7
00:00:24.760 --> 00:00:27.240
why might too few people get one?

8
00:00:27.240 --> 00:00:30.950
In this mission, you'll draw
marginal social benefit as its own

9
00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:34.120
curve and find the
socially optimal quantity.

10
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.220
Then you'll see why governments
sometimes produce goods like

11
00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.060
education and allow free access.

12
00:00:40.060 --> 00:00:43.550
Your mission: explain why
too few people get one.
